STEP 5 - UI reacts to individual param changes: implemented, then
reverted - decided against. PRM_PARAM_UI got a ui_changed flag, set by
param_msgs_process on a ui-side apply, polled by app_data_update to
push a per-param DATA_EVENT_CHANGED. Removed because: (1) it only ever
fired for value changes (name/flags apply directly to ui_params, never
through the ring buffer, so it was never the "no value/name
distinction" signal it claimed to be), (2) a fast-modulated param could
flip it every app_data_update cycle with no natural pacing, and (3)
most importantly, it wasn't protecting anything real - unlike the rt
side's val_changed (gates genuinely expensive, avoidable work: re-
sending a CLAP event, recomputation), PRM_PARAM_UI is already kept
continuously live by param_msgs_process regardless of who's watching,
so any consumer that just re-reads it on its own schedule (a redraw, a
coarser event, anything) already gets correct data for free - a per-
value push notification wasn't gating any real cost, just adding an
unpaced event source. If a future UI genuinely needs to know "something
in this instance's params changed" for its own redraw scheduling, the
already-existing per-instance params_dirty/DATA_EVENT_CHILDREN_CHANGED
(structural) is the right level to hook - polling only happens once per
app_data_update cycle either way, so per-param granularity never bought
any latency, only a saved linear rescan over an already-cheap, bounded
param count.

