Category navigation - revised design: categories become real CX tree
nodes (a VIEW app_data.c constructs - params.c's param storage stays
flat and untouched, it only gains a lifetime side-table, see below),
superseding the earlier flat-list/inline-header/CLI-local-active_
category design entirely. Once a category is an ordinary child_at
entry, the EXISTING generic navigation (helper_nav_context_scroll/
enter/exit, hover position persisted via ui_layer_nav_target_list_begin
keyed by ContextId) handles it with zero special-casing - no CLI-local
state, no l/h branching, no visible-row indirection, no inline header-
printing loop. The trigger for this revision: a CLI-local mechanism
would not get hover-position-restored-on-re-entry for free the way a
real context already does (that persistence lives in app_intrf, keyed
by ContextId) - reinventing it per-feature was the actual problem, not
just navigation ergonomics.
- What a category node actually has to answer with, in full: which
  PRM_CONTAIN it belongs to, and its FULL path prefix ("Voice/Filter").
  name() is the last segment, child_count/child_at enumerate that
  container under that prefix, id() see below. That whole descriptor is
  one pointer plus one string - the design question is purely where it
  lives, not what's in it.
- The constraint that decides everything: app_intrf_cx_create copies the
  whole DataObject into the CX and holds user_data for the CX's ENTIRE
  lifetime, and there is no callback back into the data layer when a CX
  is freed. So the descriptor's owner must outlive every CX that could
  point at it, and may only be freed at a moment when those CXs are
  guaranteed gone (CROSS-CUTTING INVARIANT). Exactly one existing thing
  has that lifetime already: the PRM_CONTAIN itself - when it dies, the
  plugin dies, the parent's child_count drops it, and cx_subtree_free
  tears the subtree down without ever dereferencing the stale user_data
  (resync pass 1 only calls data_child_at on the still-alive PARENT).
  That is the same reason param_get_handle is safe today.
- DECIDED: intern the descriptors in params.c, keyed by path. NOT a
  storage restructure - a side table hanging off PRM_CONTAIN next to the
  flat param array. params.c learns nothing about trees or path syntax,
  it only does string interning, in the same shape and with the same
  contract as the existing param_get_handle/param_handle_resolve pair:
    void *param_category_handle(PRM_CONTAIN *c, const char *path);
    bool  param_category_resolve(void *h, PRM_CONTAIN **c, int *cat_id);
    const char *param_category_path(PRM_CONTAIN *c, int cat_id);
    uint32_t param_category_uid(PRM_CONTAIN *c, int cat_id);
  resolve hands back (container, cat_id) and path/uid are ordinary
  (container, cat_id) accessors, deliberately mirroring param_handle_
  resolve + param_get_name/param_get_uid rather than inventing a second
  convention - that's what makes the cx_param_cat_* ops come out shaped
  exactly like the existing cx_param_* ones, and it lets params.c
  bounds-check cat_id instead of trusting a bare pointer. Categories are
  never individually removed, so self_cat_id is permanently valid - no
  tombstone case, unlike params.
  The intern KEY is never handed in from outside: it's always derived by
  the enumerator from param_get_category(container, val_id), sliced down
  to the current level (prefix + next segment). param_category_handle is
  called from exactly one place, cx_param_level_at, at the moment it
  decides "this row is a group whose full path is X".
  Concretely, one malloc'd descriptor per distinct path plus a growable
  array of POINTERS to them on PRM_CONTAIN, mirroring the param arrays:
    typedef struct _param_category {
        PRM_CONTAIN *self_container; // same self-ref trick as PRM_PARAM_UI
        int self_cat_id;
        uint32_t uid;             // minted from a file-static counter,
                                  // never reset, 0 reserved for "error"
        char path[...];           // the FULL path, "Voice/Filter"
    } PRM_PARAM_CATEGORY;
    PRM_PARAM_CATEGORY **categories; unsigned int num_categories;
  The pointer indirection is the whole point, for the reason already
  written at params.c:76 for rt_params/ui_params - the OUTER array is
  realloc'd on growth, which is only safe because it holds pointer
  values; a CX holds the descriptor address for its whole lifetime, so
  the descriptor itself must never move. param_category_handle is then:
  linear scan for a strcmp match, else malloc a descriptor, grow the
  outer array, mint a uid. Linear scan is fine - distinct categories per
  plugin is tens. Interning happens lazily, on the first child_at that
  needs to emit that group; params.c never parses the path, it only ever
  sees an opaque string key. All freed by param_clean_param_container.
  [main-thread] only, like param_get_handle - no rt variant.
  Rejected alternatives: an app_data.c-owned cache (needs its own GC
  pass to notice a container died, and a reused container address could
  collide with a not-yet-resynced stale entry - real bug potential, no
  gain); an owner-owned cache in clap_plugins.c (works, but would be
  rewritten in sampler.c/synth.c/plugins.c - the whole point of the
  cx_param_* rename was that this layer is owner-agnostic now).
- Interning REPLACES the path-hashing idea, and fixes a bug in it. The
  earlier "COMPUTED id via fnv1a64(full path)" bullet only reached for
  hashing because it assumed no persistent storage existed; with an
  intern table, uids can be minted monotonically from a file-static
  counter in params.c exactly like clap_plugins.c's next_param_uid, and
  stability across resyncs comes from interning (same path -> same entry
  -> same uid), not from hashing. That matters because path-only hashing
  is WRONG here: two loaded Surge XT instances both have an "Oscillator
  1" module, so fnv1a64("Oscillator 1") is identical for both, both
  category nodes get the same ContextId, and ht_set in app_intrf_cx_
  create silently overwrites one with the other. Param ids only dodge
  this because next_param_uid is one counter across every instance; a
  minted category uid dodges it the same way, a path hash would need an
  instance salt mixed in - strictly more machinery than just minting.
  Hashing stays correct for the FUTURE catalog DataChoice case below,
  where nothing persists to mint from - different problem, don't
  conflate them.
- app_data.c does NOT get messy, because there is only one enumerator:
  the plugin node and a category node ask the same question at different
  prefixes, so clap_plugin_child_at is just "list under empty prefix".
    cx_param_tree_enumerate(container, prefix):
      groups = []  # distinct next segments, dedup
      leaves = []  # val_ids
      for val_id in alive params of container:   # param_get_name != NULL
        cat = param_get_category(val_id)
        if not under(cat, prefix): continue      # cat == prefix, or
                                                 # cat starts prefix+"/"
        rest = cat minus prefix (and the "/")
        if rest empty: leaves.append(val_id)
        else:          groups.add(prefix + "/" + first segment of rest)
      return groups (sorted) ++ leaves (val_id order)
  Groups first alphabetically, THEN leaves in val_id order - val_id
  order preserves the plugin author's intended param order within a
  module, which alphabetising would destroy. child_at(idx) indexes that
  list: a group -> param_category_handle + a new cx_param_cat_ops; a
  leaf -> param_get_handle + cx_param_ops, unchanged. A category's own
  child_at resolves its handle and calls the identical function with its
  stored path, so arbitrary nesting ("User/Macros/New/...") falls out
  for free. Net effect: +4 tiny ops functions and one ops table, minus
  CxParamSortEntry, cx_param_sort_cmp and cx_param_sorted_val_ids, and
  clap_plugin_child_count/child_at shrink to two-line delegations -
  roughly break-even on size, and generic rather than CLAP-shaped.
- What the stable interned uid actually buys, stated precisely: NOT
  surviving a disappearance, but never being SEEN as disappearing in the
  first place. app_intrf_cx_resync pass 1 tests data_id(&dobj) ==
  child->data_id, so with an interned uid a category that merely
  gains/loses some params (but stays populated) compares equal, its CX
  is left untouched, and its hover entry survives. With a volatile id
  (minted fresh per child_at call, or derived from some contained param)
  the category would look removed-and-re-added on EVERY resync and
  ui_reconcile_removed would destroy the hover entry every time. Exactly
  the property param_get_uid gives params, and why param_add_param's doc
  insists a uid is never a positional index.
- Empty categories: don't GC the interned entry, but be clear that the
  CX does go and its hover entry goes with it (corrects an earlier wrong
  claim here that "the same uid on return means hover state survives" -
  it does not). ui_layer.c's ui_reconcile_removed finds the entry whose
  e->context == the removed id - that IS the category's own hover entry
  - and either clears its target list (UI_STALE_CLEAR) or deletes the
  entry outright; other entries referencing the id get ui_reconcile_
  item_policy. So a category that loses its last param loses its hover
  position, and a later return with the same uid resurrects nothing.
  That is CORRECT, not a wart - removing a plugin destroys its hover
  entry the same way, and a category with no params in it is genuinely
  gone. The only way to preserve hover across it would be to keep
  listing the empty category forever, i.e. permanently empty rows in the
  UI - not worth it. Keeping the descriptor interned anyway still buys
  no uid churn and no uid ever reused for a different path, and costs a
  pointer plus a path buffer per distinct path ever seen on that
  container, freed with the container.
- Path parsing is app_data.c's job, not params.c's: params.c keeps
  storing clap_param_info_t.module as one opaque blob, only the
  enumerator knows "/" is a separator. Keeps params.c free of syntax
  decisions. CLAP's own convention still needs confirming against the
  spec.
- MAX_CATEGORY_PATH, its OWN constant, not MAX_SHORT_NAME_LENGTH (100).
  CLAP's module is 1024, and the existing -Wformat-truncation warning on
  the category snprintf becomes load-bearing once paths are parsed: a
  truncated path can MERGE two distinct categories (and, since the path
  is the intern key, merge their CX nodes too), not just uglify a name.
  Size it deliberately.
- Why the category uid counter sits in params.c while PARAM uids are
  minted by the owner: the minting site follows WHO DECIDES IDENTITY.
  An owner mints a param uid because only it can do survivor-matching
  against its own external key (clap_plugins.c:730 - clap_plug_find_
  val_id_by_clap_id on param_info.id, then param_get_uid for a survivor
  / a fresh uid for a new one); params.c has no idea what "the same
  param" means for CLAP. A category's identity key IS its path string,
  which params.c can compare directly, so params.c is the only thing
  that can tell new from existing - and there's no owner in scope
  anyway, param_category_handle is called from the owner-agnostic
  enumerator. Principled, but see the next item - it's a symptom.
