- Optimisation, NOT a prerequisite - two independent quadratics stacked,
  fixed in different places. Neither matters at today's CLAP param
  counts; measure before doing either.
  (a) app_intrf_cx_resync's nested scan, the bigger and more GENERAL
      one: pass 1 loops over existing CX children and re-calls data_
      child_at over every data child for each, pass 2 mirrors it - ~n^2
      child_at calls per level before the data layer does any work.
      Fix once in app_intrf.c by materialising the data children's
      ContextIds into a temp array first (n calls), then running both
      passes against that array; pass 2 re-fetches the DataObject only
      for ids it actually creates (rare). ~15 lines in one function, no
      new invariants, and it benefits EVERY context type, not just
      params. Do this one first if anything makes resync hot.
  (b) each child_at re-enumerating the whole container, O(total) per
      call: a one-entry memo in app_data.c is enough, because the
      access pattern is always child_count(prefix) then child_at(0..n-1)
      on the SAME prefix. Hit requires container + prefix + generation
      all to match; generation is a new counter on PRM_CONTAIN bumped by
      param_add_param and params_container_resync and ONLY those, since
      a row list depends solely on which params are alive and on their
      categories (Operation_ChangeName can't affect it - names aren't in
      the row list - and a category is immutable after add).
  With (a) alone a level costs n * O(total); with (a)+(b), O(total) once
  per level per frame.
- Namespace: DATA_NS_PARAM_CATEGORY, generic from day one - the same
  way DATA_NS_PARAM already is, now that params.c mints every uid from
  one counter.

